    "granted_by": "Maintainer decision, 2026-08-23. 'GO for Recovery Continuation Attempt 6. I reviewed 01be685. The Attempt-5 checkpoint-consumer defect is closed at the right scope. trained_model_dir(out_dir) now resolves the producer's actual contract, out_dir/checkpoints/latest.txt -> out_dir/checkpoints/<tag>/model, and run_probe() delegates to that resolver rather than performing its own path arithmetic. The two post-paid-training failure modes are separately fail-closed. The correction to test_phase_a_stages1_5_execute.py is accepted and important: the harness was not absent; its fake trainer had encoded the defective consumer layout. The fake now emits the same checkpoint tree as Trainer.save_checkpoint(), so the existing end-to-end $0 path can actually catch this defect class. Do not add any further checkpoint abstraction or trained-only retention machinery.' Against last tested implementation 01be685, which is also the pre-authorization HEAD. Since attempt 5 one closure landed and was accepted: THE PROBE CHECKPOINT CONSUMER. Attempt 5 trained its first rung-1 probe for 61.7 minutes and then died reading it, because run_probe read out_dir/latest.txt and out_dir/<tag>/model while Trainer.save_checkpoint writes out_dir/checkpoints/latest.txt and checkpoints/<tag>/model -- one writer, two consumers, and only this one wrong. The repair is a driver-local resolver plus a targeted regression over a synthetic real-layout tree. Critically, the earlier claim that no $0 path executed the line was WRONG and the truth was worse: tests/pod/test_phase_a_stages1_5_execute.py does execute run_probe end to end, and passed only because its fake trainer wrote the layout the DRIVER expected rather than the trainer's. The full suite went red the moment the driver was corrected. The fake now writes the real tree, and reintroducing the driver defect against it makes that harness fail 1+14, so the defect class is now catchable at $0. Verified on the current tree: full suite 2176 passed / 12 skipped, +4 with the skip count unchanged; frozen-asset verifier passed with no problems; 5 mutations each turning a passing state into a failing one, including the 15-minute end-to-end one. The continuation harness digest moved 95cf336d -> 0dbf1272.\n\nCovers: One (1) launch of the AutoInitializer Phase-A RECOVERY CONTINUATION, attempt 6, on a single NVIDIA L40S at no more than $0.99/h. It imports the verified Attempt-12 Stage-1 result -- the five preserved selected leaves, in the committed selected order, re-identified from the bytes that actually arrive on the pod -- and runs recovery Stages 2 through 5 under the frozen science, the existing watchdog, soft-stop, hard-stop, artifact-gate and provider-confirmed teardown behaviour. Everything ahead of Stage 2 is already demonstrated on paid hardware and is not revisited: the transport (attempts 3 and 4), Stage 0 attestation and the strict Stage-1 import with control measurement and admission (attempts 4 and 5), and the release/headroom contracts (attempt 5, freed_allocated_bytes 8,101,709,824 and 43.87 GiB free against the 43.65 GiB gate). Attempt 5 also trained one rung-1 probe in 61.7 minutes against the 61.55 priced; that probe was lost with its pod and is NOT resumable scientific work, so this attempt starts recovery normally from the preserved Stage-1 inputs and retrains it. Authorized pricing is DERIVED from continuation_budget(), not written: expected $14.9233, per-launch hard ceiling $16.7456, which is also the hard cap. The beam search is not run, not reconstructed, and is structurally unreachable from this harness.\n\nCumulative spend at approval $215.6837 against a cumulative cap of $234.00.\n\nDoes NOT authorize: Adding any further checkpoint abstraction, or trained-but-unscored cross-session retention machinery. Rerunning or redesigning Stage 1. Revisiting the transport. Altering the verified memory-release or headroom contracts, including RECOVERY_TRAINER_BYTES, require_released and require_headroom, and in particular lowering any threshold to make a launch succeed or avoid a refusal. Changing the frozen recovery recipe, KD chunk, microbatch, dtype, optimizer, trainer implementation or the selection rules. Repairing and relaunching on a live pod. A beam search, a fresh Stage 1, or any reconstruction of the search. Re-ranking or re-selecting the five leaves, or changing their committed order. Retraining the permanent controls. Measuring the canonical control more than once or on anything but the frozen Stage-1 suite. Any new canary, rehearsal, readiness, measurement, calibration, architecture, pricing or harness expansion. Any additional storage cleanup, including the 32 sole-copy remote objects totalling 69.671 GiB or the two protected wheelhouses. Increasing the cumulative cap. Any follow-on or automatically started session. Reopening the frozen science: science plan 02be33b9, session plan 9377a2dc, Stage-3 protocol 250f72ef, equivalence interval 0.011695296982299022, feasibility floor 0.3, tokenizer 7781771acc3798ee, recovery fingerprint ab0d8cfd, the seed and conditional-sc policy, the operator ids and declared mathematics. Reusing any spent grant or authorization from attempts 1 through 5. Any other paid session. Treating the remaining cumulative headroom as authorization beyond this one launch.\n\nGrant document: logs/autoinit_recovery_continuation_attempt6_grant.json sha256 e286753b2c9661d6a609c3eefabc85d4d8e76c723af0ce7e51f383e73c9c7bb5",
